.TH FROM2TO 1 "2026-08-21" "from2to 0.4.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
from2to \- convert between number bases and byte encodings
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B [le]<from>2[le]<to>
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIVALUE\fR]...
.br
.B f2t
\fIFROM\fR \fITO\fR [\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fIVALUE\fR]...
.br
.B whatbase
[\fIOPTION\fR]... [\fITEXT\fR]
.br
.B from2to
\fISUBCOMMAND\fR [\fIOPTION\fR]...
.SH DESCRIPTION
One binary answers to about sixty-five names. The name it was invoked under picks the
conversion: \fBhex2bin\fR converts hex to binary, \fBdec2char\fR converts decimal
code points to text, \fBchar2b64\fR converts text to base64.
.PP
Values come from the command line, or from standard input when none are given.
Output streams as it is produced, so a slow producer shows up live; the one
exception is base64 and base32 \fIoutput\fR, which must see the whole input
before it can place its padding.
.PP
Run
.B from2to install
once to create the per\-conversion commands. Without them, everything is still
reachable through \fBf2t\fR.
.SH FORMATS
.TP
.B bin \fR(\fBb\fR, \fBbinary\fR)
Base 2.
.TP
.B oct \fR(\fBo\fR, \fBoctal\fR)
Base 8.
.TP
.B dec \fR(\fBd\fR, \fBdecimal\fR, \fBint\fR)
Base 10.
.TP
.B hex \fR(\fBh\fR, \fBhexadecimal\fR)
Base 16.
.TP
.B char \fR(\fBascii\fR, \fBchr\fR, \fBtext\fR, \fBstr\fR, \fBraw\fR, \fButf8\fR)
Text: Unicode scalars, or raw bytes.
.TP
.B b64 \fR(\fBbase64\fR), \fBb64url\fR, \fBb32 \fR(\fBbase32\fR), \fBb32hex\fR
The RFC 4648 encodings.
.TP
.B esc \fR(\fBescape\fR, \fBfhex\fR, \fBxhex\fR)
Escaped hex: the \fB\ex48\ex65\fR notation payloads are written in. Every byte is
escaped on the way out, so the output round-trips and never leaves a literal
backslash ambiguous. On the way in, the short escapes \fB\en \er \et \e0 \e\e\fR are
understood and literal text passes through, because
.B GET /\er\en
is what you actually copy out of an exploit script. \fB\-\-strict\fR accepts
nothing but \fB\exNN\fR.
.TP
.I N
Any bare radix from 2 to 36, for use with \fBf2t\fR.
.PP
A conversion between two powers of two is pure bit regrouping, so the digits you
wrote carry real width information and leading zeros are preserved:
.B hex2bin 0f
is \fB00001111\fR, not \fB1111\fR. Base 10 has no such structure, so decimal
results are always bare.
.PP
.B hex2char
reads bytes, because hex digits come in byte\-sized pairs.
.B dec2char
reads Unicode code points, because decimal digits do not.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-width " " \fIN\fR
Pad each result to \fIN\fR digits.
.TP
.BR \-W ", " \-\-no\-width
Drop the natural digit width and emit bare numbers.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-group " " \fIN\fR
Group output digits in runs of \fIN\fR.
.TP
.B \-\-word " " \fIBYTES\fR
Bytes per word for a byte-order swap. A number is zero-extended on the left to
a whole number of words; a run of bytes that is not a whole number of words is
an error unless \fB\-\-keep\-going\fR is given. Implies \fB\-\-bytes\fR. This
counts \fIbytes of the value\fR, where \fB\-\-width\fR counts \fIdigits of the
output\fR \- the two are independent and may both be given.
.TP
.BR \-u ", " \-\-upper
Uppercase the output digits.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-prefix
Prefix results with \fB0x\fR, \fB0b\fR or \fB0o\fR.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-delim " " \fIS\fR
Separator between results. The escapes \fB\\n\fR, \fB\\t\fR, \fB\\r\fR and
\fB\\0\fR are understood.
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-join
Run results together with no separator.
.TP
.BR \-b ", " \-\-bytes
Treat \fBchar\fR as raw bytes.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-codepoint
Treat \fBchar\fR as Unicode code points.
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-keep\-going
Skip tokens that fail instead of stopping. A bad digit is otherwise an error.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress warnings.
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-no\-newline
Do not add a trailing newline.
.TP
.B \-\-strict
Reject \fB0x\fR/\fB0b\fR/\fB0o\fR prefixes, \fB_\fR spacers and unpadded base64.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.SH INPUT
Numbers may be separated by whitespace, newlines or commas, and may carry a
\fB0x\fR, \fB0b\fR or \fB0o\fR prefix or \fB_\fR spacers. \fB\-\-strict\fR turns
that leniency off.
.PP
For \fBchar\fR input, whitespace is data rather than a separator \- except for
one trailing newline arriving from a pipe, which is dropped so that
.B echo hi | char2hex
does the obvious thing.
.SH BYTE ORDER
Either half of a conversion name may carry an \fBle\fR or \fBbe\fR prefix:
.B lehex2dec
reads little-endian hex,
.B hex2lebin
writes little-endian binary, and
.B lehex2ledec
does both. \fBbe\fR is the default written down, so it changes nothing; it
exists so a script can be explicit rather than rely on a default it cannot see.
Only the \fBle\fR names are installed as commands, but every combination
resolves through
.BR f2t ,
so \fBf2t leb642hex\fR works whether or not anything was installed.
.PP
A byte order only means something when the byte width is known, and that width
comes from how the \fIsource\fR wrote the value:
.TP
.B char, b64, b32
The bytes are the input, so the run is its own width. The swap covers the whole
run, not one line at a time \- these conversions read to end of input.
.TP
.B hex, bin
Byte-aligned, so the digits you wrote are a byte count. \fBlehex2dec 0d0c0b0a\fR
is 168496141, and \fBlehex2dec ff\fR is 255, because a single byte has no byte
order.
.TP
.B dec, oct, and every other base
No byte width of their own \- 255 is one byte and 256 is two, so the word size
would depend on the value. These are an error until \fB\-\-word\fR says how
wide a word is.
.PP
Numbers swap per token: \fBlehex2hex 0102 0304\fR is two results, not a
re-reading of four bytes. An odd hex digit count is an error rather than a
guess, since there is no byte boundary to swap on; \fB\-\-keep\-going\fR
downgrades that to a warning, as it does for any other bad token.
.PP
.B \-\-word
groups the bytes into fixed-size words and reverses within each, leaving the
order of the words alone \- the case of a hexdump taken off a 32-bit
little-endian machine:
.PP
.RS 4
.nf
$ lehex2hex \-\-word 4 0d0c0b0a78563412
0a0b0c0d12345678
$ dec2lehex \-\-word 4 1234
d2040000
.fi
.RE
.PP
Two notes worth having in advance. \fB\-W\fR strips the leading zeros of the
\fIrendered\fR result, which after a swap are the zeros that used to be
trailing, so \fBlehex2hex \-W 0100\fR is \fB1\fR rather than \fB0001\fR. And
reversing UTF-8 bytes is not reversing characters: \fBchar2lehex \['e]\fR gives
\fBa9c3\fR, which is no longer valid UTF-8.
.SH IDENTIFYING A FORMAT
.B whatbase
takes text and works out what it plausibly is, for when the format is not known
in advance. It is also available as
.BR "from2to detect" .
.TP
.BR \-a ", " \-\-all
.TP
.BR \-w ", " \-\-why
Show how it decided: the confidence scores, the evidence for the winning
reading, and the command that finishes the job. Without it the report is just
the format and what the input says.
Show every candidate, not just the likely ones.
.TP
.BR \-d ", " \-\-decode
Print what the best guess decodes to, and nothing else.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Print just the format name, for scripts.
.TP
.B \-\-to " " \fIFMT\fR
With \fB\-\-decode\fR, the format to decode into. Defaults to \fBchar\fR.
.PP
Three questions decide the answer: whether the alphabet admits the input, whether
it is the narrowest alphabet that does, and whether the payload decodes to
anything meaningful. The score is a confidence and only means something next to
the other candidates.
.SH SUBCOMMANDS
.TP
.B install
Create the per\-conversion commands. They are links to this one binary, so the
whole set costs no extra disk space.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dir " " \fIPATH\fR
Where to put them. Defaults to the directory holding this binary.
.TP
.B \-\-method " " \fIHOW\fR
One of \fBsymlink\fR, \fBhardlink\fR or \fBcopy\fR. The default is a symlink on
Unix, because it keeps working when the binary is replaced by an upgrade, and a
hardlink on Windows, where symlinks need Developer Mode or an elevated prompt.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be created and change nothing.
.TP
.B \-\-force
Replace existing files that are not ours. Without it, a name already taken by
another program is reported and left alone.
.RE
.TP
.B uninstall
Remove the commands again. Files that are not ours are left alone; both
directions recognize their own work by a marker compiled into the binary, so any
version can clean up after any other.
.RS
.TP
.B \-\-dir " " \fIPATH\fR
Where to look. Defaults to the directory holding this binary.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Show what would be removed and change nothing.
.RE
.TP
.B list
Print every command name and the conversion it performs.
.TP
.B detect
The same thing as \fBwhatbase\fR, for use before the commands are installed.
.SH NUMERIC SHORTHANDS
Using \fB2\fR as the word "to", \fB1628\fR reads as 16\-to\-8. These exist only
for bases 2, 8, 10 and 16, and that limit is deliberate: with \fB2\fR serving as
both a base and the separator, those sixteen names each have exactly one valid
reading, while \fB216\fR would be both 2\-to\-16 and 21\-to\-6.
.PP
They are understood by \fBf2t\fR but are deliberately not installed as commands,
since a file called \fB822\fR sitting in a directory on \fBPATH\fR is a poor
citizen. Write
.B f2t 1628 ff
instead.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
.TP
.B 1
A conversion failed, nothing could be identified, or \fBinstall\fR found a name
already taken by something else.
.TP
.B 2
No arguments were given under the binary's own name, so help was printed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Convert a hex byte to binary, keeping the written width:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ hex2bin 0f
00001111
.RE
.fi
.PP
Decode a stream of decimal code points as it arrives:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ nc example.com 4000 | dec2char
flag{d3c0d3d_1n_0n3_p1p3}
.RE
.fi
.PP
Identify an unknown string and decode it in one step:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ whatbase \-d bDNhcm5fdGgzX3IwcDM1
l3arn_th3_r0p35
.RE
.fi
.PP
Use the detected format in a pipeline:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ f2t "$(whatbase \-q "$blob")" char "$blob"
.RE
.fi
.PP
Any base from 2 to 36:
.PP
.nf
.RS
$ f2t 36 10 zz
1295
.RE
.fi
